#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM8036", unit: "U5")
#let items = (
p(1,"E","U5.1 Pi formation",[State the data needed to form $Pi_(x:A)B(x)$ in context $Gamma$.],[The codomain is checked in an extended context.],[It may mention the new variable $x$.],[One needs $Gamma tack.r A$ type and $Gamma,x:A tack.r B(x)$ type. With levels $A:U_i$ and $B(x):U_j$, the product lies in $U_(max(i,j))$. The body family is not required to be constant.],[State the extended context and level rule.]),
p(2,"E","U5.1 Dependent application",[If $f:Pi_(x:A)B(x)$ and $a:A$, what is the type of $f a$?],[Substitute the actual argument into the family.],[The result lies over $a$.],[The type is $B(a)$, formally $B(x)[x:=a]$. Using an unrelated fiber $B(b)$ would require additional equality evidence and transport.],[Show the dependency in the result type.]),
p(3,"E","U5.2 Sigma introduction",[What must be supplied to construct a term of $Sigma_(x:A)B(x)$?],[The second component depends on the first.],[Supply $a:A$ and then $b:B(a)$.],[The pair $(a,b)$ inhabits the dependent sum when $a:A$ and $b:B(a)$. An arbitrary $b:B(c)$ cannot be paired with $a$ unless the needed conversion or transport is provided.],[State the exact fiber for the second component.]),
p(4,"E","U5.2 Sigma projections",[State the types and computation rules for the projections of $p:Sigma_(x:A)B(x)$.],[The second projection's type mentions the first.],[Evaluate both projections on $(a,b)$.],[The first projection has type $A$, and the second has type $B(pi_1(p))$. On a constructor pair, $pi_1(a,b) equiv a$ and $pi_2(a,b) equiv b$, with the second type converting to $B(a)$.],[Include dependent typing, not just projection equations.]),
p(5,"C","U5.1 Dependent identity",[Derive the type of $lambda A.lambda x.x$ with $A:U_0$. Determine the level of its type.],[The inner identity is small, but the outer domain is a universe.],[Use $U_0:U_1$.],[Under $A:U_0,x:A$, the variable rule gives $x:A$. Abstraction twice gives $Pi_(A:U_0)(A->A)$. The inner arrow is in $U_0$, but the outer Pi has domain $U_0$, which belongs to $U_1$; hence the complete type lies in $U_1$.],[Do not infer that a quantification over all small types is itself small.]),
p(6,"C","U5.2 Dependent pair map",[Given $f:A->A'$ and $g:Pi_(x:A)(B(x)->B'(f x))$, construct a map $Sigma_(x:A)B(x)->Sigma_(y:A')B'(y)$.],[Eliminate the input pair.],[Send $(a,b)$ to $(f a,g a b)$.],[On a pair $(a,b)$, form first component $f a:A'$ and second component $g a b:B'(f a)$. The Sigma introduction rule therefore gives the required output pair. Pair elimination extends this definition to every input.],[Check the second component against the transformed first component.]),
p(7,"C","U5.1 Dependent composition",[Given $f:Pi_(x:A)B(x)$ and $g:Pi_(x:A)(B(x)->C(x))$, derive a term of $Pi_(x:A)C(x)$.],[Work under an arbitrary $x:A$.],[Apply $g$ at the same index as $f$.],[The term is $lambda x.g x(f x)$. In context $x:A$, dependent application gives $f x:B(x)$ and $g x:B(x)->C(x)$, then ordinary application gives $C(x)$. Abstraction closes the derivation.],[Keep both functions at the same index.]),
p(8,"C","U5.2 Existential evidence",[Explain the computational difference between $Sigma_(n:"Nat")P(n)$ and $Pi_(n:"Nat")P(n)$, and give their respective elimination uses.],[One stores a witness; one accepts an arbitrary input.],[Project a Sigma; apply a Pi.],[A Sigma term contains a particular number and evidence about that number. Its projections yield $n:"Nat"$ and evidence of $P(n)$. A Pi term supplies evidence of $P(n)$ for each requested $n$ and is eliminated by application to a chosen number. A single witness does not establish all instances.],[Use the exact types of the extracted evidence.]),
p(9,"C","U5.2 Constant families",[Show that Sigma and Pi over constant families reproduce ordinary products and arrows.],[Write their introduction, elimination, and computation rules.],[The substitution into a constant family changes nothing.],[If $B(x)=B$ independently of $x$, a Sigma pair has components $a:A,b:B$, its projections have types $A,B$, and their beta rules are those of $A times B$. A Pi term is an abstraction returning $B$, application returns $B$, and its beta rule is ordinary function substitution. Thus these are the dependent constructions specialized to constant families, subject to the chosen eta rules of the theory.],[Do not silently add an eta equality absent from the chosen presentation.]),
p(10,"A","U5.2 Constructive choice",[Construct a function from $Pi_(x:A)Sigma_(y:B(x))R(x,y)$ to $Sigma_(f:Pi_(x:A)B(x))Pi_(x:A)R(x,f x)$. Explain why this does not introduce classical choice.],[Each input already provides a pair at every $x$.],[Define $f$ by first projection and its evidence by second projection.],[For input $h$, let $f=lambda x.pi_1(h x)$ and $r=lambda x.pi_2(h x)$. The second projection has type $R(x,pi_1(h x))$, which converts to $R(x,f x)$ by the beta rule for $f$. Hence $(f,r)$ has the target Sigma type. The premise is a function returning witness-and-proof pairs, not a mere truncated or classical assertion of existence; the construction reorganizes data already supplied.],[Verify conversion of the dependent result and explain what the premise contains.]),
p(11,"A","U5.2 Dependent currying",[Construct maps between $Pi_(p:Sigma_(x:A)B(x))C(pi_1(p),pi_2(p))$ and $Pi_(x:A)Pi_(y:B(x))C(x,y)$. Verify their computation on pair inputs.],[Evaluate an uncurried function on a constructed pair.],[For the reverse, eliminate the input pair.],[Send $h$ to $lambda x.lambda y.h(x,y)$. Conversely send $k$ to the function defined on $(x,y)$ by $k x y$. Starting from $h$, the round trip at $(x,y)$ computes to $h(x,y)$; starting from $k$, the other round trip applied to $x,y$ computes to $k x y$. These pointwise equations follow from Pi and Sigma beta rules. An internal equality of the functions is not being inferred without function extensionality.],[Check the dependent codomain in each direction and limit the equality claim.]),
p(12,"A","U5.1 Context dependency",[Explain why exchanging $x:A,y:B(x)$ into $y:B(x),x:A$ is generally invalid, although weakening by a fresh declaration is often admissible. Give a concrete family example.],[A context must be well formed from left to right.],[Use $n:"Nat",v:"Vec"(D,n)$.],[For fixed $D:U_0$, consider $n:"Nat",v:"Vec"(D,n)$. The declaration of $v$ requires the previously available $n$ to form its type. Placing $v:"Vec"(D,n)$ first leaves $n$ unbound, so the context is not well formed. A fresh independent declaration can instead be inserted where all declarations remain well formed, and terms may be weakened accordingly. Exchange is admissible only when the dependency conditions permit it, unlike arbitrary exchange in a simple-type context.],[Identify a formation failure, not merely an unconventional order.]),
)
